I spend most of my working hours thinking about how to make other people's phones ring. Ads, funnels, search, the whole machine. So it surprises me a little to admit that the favorite thing I do all month has no funnel attached to it at all.

It's an interview. Me, a founder, a recorder running, and a couple of hours to hear the real story of something they built. We call the series Built in San Diego, and when people ask me what it is, the honest answer is that it's a free feature article about a local business. No pitch. No catch. We tell the story well and hand it over.

The marketing part of my brain knows exactly why that's good for Revvia. But that's not why I love it. I love it because it feels like a return to how business used to work, before every handshake became a lead and every coffee became a discovery call. Value first. People first. The work speaks, and the rest tends to follow.

## Value First, With Nothing Attached

The whole design of Built in San Diego is that it isn't about me. The spotlight points entirely at the person across the table. That doesn't sound radical until you've spent twenty years watching "adding value" get used as a tactic, a thing you do to someone so they'll owe you one.

Dr. Mark O'Brien is a good example of why I started this. He runs Coastal Internal Medicine, an independent practice up in Vista, and he's been practicing medicine for more than thirty-five years. In an era when most doctors have been absorbed into giant systems, he's still his own man, still taking the kind of time with patients that the big groups cut out of the schedule a long time ago. Sitting with him, I didn't feel like I was marketing anything. I felt like I was getting to document something worth documenting: a guy quietly doing excellent work and still, after all these years, in love with it.

What I loved most was watching him realize his story was interesting to somebody. Founders and operators who are heads-down and great at what they do almost never get asked the real version of their story. Getting to hand him that was the whole reward. If a new patient ever finds him because of the piece, wonderful, but that was never the price of admission.

## The Relationships You Don't Go Looking For

Here's the part nobody can engineer: the relationships that come out of this when you aren't fishing for anything.

Joe Phillips is the one I think about. Joe runs peer advisory groups for CEOs here in San Diego, which means he spends his days in rooms full of founders working through the hardest parts of running a company. We met in the orbit of all this, two people who care about the same thing from different angles, and somewhere along the way Joe stopped being a contact and became a genuine friend. Not a networking friend. A real one. The kind you call when you're chewing on a hard decision and you want someone who actually gets it.

I didn't go looking for that. You can't. The old networking advice to "give value and the relationships will come" is true, but it only works if you mean the giving part and let go of the scoreboard. When you do, the good people find each other. That's not a growth hack. It's just how it's supposed to go.

## Honoring the Thing Someone Built

Every so often an interview moves me in a way I don't expect. Dr. Trish Stanley did that.

Trish built one of the leading couples therapy practices in San Diego, and she built it around a stubborn, unfashionable idea: that therapy should actually change something. Not an hour a week of venting into the void, but real movement, people getting unstuck and staying unstuck. Listening to her talk about the couples she's helped pull back from the edge, and about where she wants to take the work next, I stopped thinking about the article entirely. I was just moved by it.

That's the thing I underestimated when I started Built in San Diego. There is something almost sacred about work a person has poured years of their life into, and most of the time nobody stops to honor it. I'm a former pastor, so maybe I'm wired to notice, but a person's work is rarely just a business. It's their convictions made visible. Getting to put a frame around that and say, look what this person made, is a privilege I don't take lightly.

## What This Says About How I Run Revvia

I'll be honest about the business side, because the founders reading this are probably sizing me up, and that's fair.

Built in San Diego is, among other things, the truest sales material Revvia has. Not because of the leads it generates, though it generates them. Because it shows you how we operate when there's nothing in it for us. We lead with value because that isn't a clever front-of-funnel trick for us. It's the actual temperament of the company. If I'll spend two hours and a real editorial effort on a business I may never work with, for free, you can make a reasonable guess about how I'll treat you once you're paying me to care.

That's the whole pitch, really. The agency world taught a lot of founders to expect polish on the outside and indifference underneath. I'd rather you judge us by what we do when nobody's keeping score.

## Marketing How It Should Be

So no, the best marketing I do isn't marketing. It's sitting across from someone who built something real and giving their story the telling it deserves, with no invoice attached. It reminds me every month why I got into this in the first place: that business, at its best, is just people making valuable things and rooting for each other out loud.

If you're a San Diego founder with a story worth telling, I'd love to hear it. That isn't a sales line. It's an open invitation. Come be in [the series](https://revvia.com/journal/built-in-san-diego), whether or not we ever do business. The feature is on the house.

God Bless,

Tim Holt - Revvia Marketing

## Frequently Asked Questions

**What is Built in San Diego?**
Built in San Diego is a free editorial feature series by Revvia Marketing that profiles local San Diego founders and business owners. Each article tells the story of how someone built their company, at no cost and with no obligation to the business featured.

**How much does it cost to be featured in Built in San Diego?**
Nothing. Features are free. Revvia produces each article as a value-first way to showcase local entrepreneurs, and being featured does not require becoming a Revvia client.

**Who runs Built in San Diego?**
It is written and produced by Tim Holt, founder and CEO of Revvia Marketing, a founder-led growth studio based in Carlsbad, California.

**How do you get featured in Built in San Diego?**
San Diego founders and business owners can reach out to Revvia directly to be considered. The series focuses on people who have built something distinctive, regardless of industry or size.

**What does Revvia Marketing do?**
Revvia is a San Diego growth studio offering paid ads, SEO and generative engine optimization (AI visibility), branding, social media, and in-house creative production, run by the founder and delivered in person. It works month to month with no long-term contracts.

**Is Built in San Diego just marketing for Revvia?**
It builds relationships and reputation, so in that sense yes. But features are given freely with no strings. The business featured owes nothing and is never pitched as a condition of the article.
